# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 3 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "gw_unqualified ∧ ¬talents_unqualified ∧ ¬fortune_unqualified"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 has "gw_unqualified ∧ ¬◇talents_unqualified ∧ ¬◇fortune_unqualified"; they differ when only "a good will can be called good without qualification" and "possibly, the gifts of fortune (power, riches, honour, health, happiness) can be called good without qualification" are true, which the first allows; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 has "gw_unqualified"
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S1 is read kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 with no modality; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "a good will can be called good without qualification" and "possibly, the gifts of fortune (power, riches, honour, health, happiness) can be called good without qualification" are true and "the talents of the mind and qualities of temperament can be called good without qualification", "the gifts of fortune (power, riches, honour, health, happiness) can be called good without qualification" and "possibly, the talents of the mind and qualities of temperament can be called good without qualification" are false. Is S1, "Nothing can possibly be conceived in the world, or even out of it, which can be called good, without qualification, except a good will.", true then?
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "¬good_will → ◇talents_bad"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 has "□(¬good_will → talents_bad)"; they differ when only "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" is true, which the first allows; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 has "◇(¬good_will ∧ talents_bad)"
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "possibly, (it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will and the gifts of nature become extremely bad and mischievous)" are true and "necessarily, (if it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will, then the gifts of nature become extremely bad and mischievous)" and "possibly, the gifts of nature become extremely bad and mischievous" are false. Is S2, "Intelligence, wit, judgement, and the other talents of the mind, however they may be named, or courage, resolution, perseverance, as qualities of temperament, are undoubtedly good and desirable in many respects; but these gifts of nature may also become extremely bad and mischievous if the will which is to make use of them, and which, therefore, constitutes what is called character, is not good.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "¬good_will → fortune_pride"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 has "□(¬good_will → fortune_pride)"; they differ when only "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the gifts of fortune inspire pride and often presumption" are true, which the first allows; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 has "◇(¬good_will ∧ fortune_pride)"
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 with no modality; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the gifts of fortune inspire pride and often presumption" are true and "possibly, (it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will and the gifts of fortune inspire pride and often presumption)" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will, then the gifts of fortune inspire pride and often presumption)" are false. Is S4, "Power, riches, honour, even health, and the general well-being and contentment with one's condition which is called happiness, inspire pride, and often presumption, if there is not a good will to correct the influence of these on the mind, and with this also to rectify the whole principle of acting and adapt it to its end.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "¬good_will → ¬pleases_spectator"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 have "□(¬good_will → ¬pleases_spectator)"; they differ when only "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator" are true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 with no modality; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator" are true and "necessarily, (if it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will, then it is not so that the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator)" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will, then it is not so that the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator)" are false. Is S5, "The sight of a being who is not adorned with a single feature of a pure and good will, enjoying unbroken prosperity, can never give pleasure to an impartial rational spectator.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "worthy → pleases_spectator"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 has "□(worthy → pleases_spectator)"; they differ when only "the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator" and "the prospering being is worthy of happiness" are true, which the first allows; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 has "◇talents_bad → ¬talents_unqualified" and "◇fortune_pride → ¬fortune_unqualified" and "□(worthy → pleases_spectator)" [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 has "worthy → good_will"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 have "□(worthy → good_will)"; they differ when only "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the prospering being is worthy of happiness" are true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 with no modality; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will" and "the prospering being is worthy of happiness" are true and "necessarily, (if the prospering being is worthy of happiness, then the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will)" and "necessarily, (if the prospering being is worthy of happiness, then the will which makes use of these gifts is a good will)" are false. Is the conclusion, "Thus a good will appears to constitute the indispensable condition even of being worthy of happiness.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3 take for granted "necessarily, (if the prospering being is worthy of happiness, then the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator)"; kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if the prospering being is worthy of happiness, then the sight of the prospering being gives pleasure to an impartial rational spectator)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
